import { plantHormonesQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/plant-hormones';

const toc = [
  { id: 'messengers', label: 'Chemical messengers in plants' },
  { id: 'five', label: 'The five main hormone groups' },
  { id: 'apical', label: 'Auxin and apical dominance' },
  { id: 'gibberellins', label: 'Gibberellins' },
  { id: 'cytokinins', label: 'Cytokinins' },
  { id: 'aba', label: 'Abscisic acid and drought' },
  { id: 'ethylene', label: 'Ethylene and ripening' },
  { id: 'interactions', label: 'Hormones work together' },
  { id: 'dose', label: 'Dose, tissue and timing' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Why it matters' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the simulator simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 30.6: Plant Sensory Systems and Responses. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/30-6-plant-sensory-systems-and-responses' },
  { text: 'Taiz, L., Zeiger, E., Møller, I. M., & Murphy, A. (2015). Plant Physiology and Development (6th ed.). Sinauer Associates.' },
  { text: 'Davies, P. J. (Ed.). (2010). Plant Hormones: Biosynthesis, Signal Transduction, Action! (3rd ed.). Springer.' },
  { text: 'Thimann, K. V., & Skoog, F. (1933). Studies on the growth hormone of plants. III. The inhibiting action of the growth substance on bud development. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 19(7), 714–716.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.19.7.714' },
  { text: 'Skoog, F., & Miller, C. O. (1957). Chemical regulation of growth and organ formation in plant tissues cultured in vitro. Symposia of the Society for Experimental Biology, 11, 118–130.' },
  { text: 'Peng, J., et al. (1999). “Green revolution” genes encode mutant gibberellin response modulators. Nature, 400, 256–261.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1038/22307' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="botany"
  topic="plant-hormones"
  description="Interactive plant hormone simulator: change auxin, gibberellin, cytokinin, ABA and ethylene levels, remove the shoot tip, add drought, and watch growth, apical dominance, stomata, ripening and tissue culture respond."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={plantHormonesQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Plants have no nervous system, yet a pine knows which way to grow, a bean shuts its stomata in a drought and a banana
      can ripen its neighbours. Much of this is done by a handful of hormones. Turn each one up or down, cut off a shoot tip,
      dry out the soil, and watch a plant, its cells and four classic experiments respond.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <HormoneSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>In <strong>Whole plant</strong>, the plant on the right grows with your hormone levels and the one on the left is untreated. Move a slider or choose a preset, then press <strong>Grow new plants</strong> to compare from the start.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Cut off the shoot tip</strong> to remove the main source of auxin, then try again with <strong>Auxin paste on the cut stem</strong> turned on.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Drought</strong> and tap a leaf to check its stomata. Choose a hormone under <strong>Show hormone movement</strong> to follow it through the plant.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Cells</strong>, see how hormones change cell division and cell elongation, and drag the line on the auxin chart.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Lab tests</strong>, watch stomata, ripening fruit, a cereal grain and a tissue culture respond to the same sliders. <strong>Step</strong> moves forward a little at a time; <strong>Reset</strong> restores everything.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="messengers">Chemical messengers in plants</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>plant hormone</strong> (or plant growth regulator) is a chemical made in small amounts in one part of a plant
    that changes growth or development, either in the same tissue or somewhere else. Unlike animals, plants have no
    glands: almost any living cell can make hormones, and they travel from cell to cell, in the xylem and phloem, or as a
    gas through the air. Each hormone has many effects, and the response depends on the tissue, the concentration and the
    other hormones present.
  </p>

  <h2 id="five">The five main hormone groups</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Hormone</th><th>Main effects</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Auxin</strong> (made in shoot tips)</td><td>Stem cell elongation, apical dominance, new roots, tropisms</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gibberellins</strong> (GA)</td><td>Stem elongation, seed germination, bolting, fruit growth</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cytokinins</strong> (made in root tips)</td><td>Cell division, bud and shoot growth, delay leaf ageing</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Abscisic acid</strong> (ABA)</td><td>Closes stomata in drought, keeps seeds and buds dormant</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ethylene</strong> (a gas)</td><td>Fruit ripening, leaf and fruit fall, ageing</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Other signals, such as brassinosteroids, jasmonates, salicylic acid and strigolactones, are also important. Auxin’s
    role in bending towards light and gravity is explored in the <a href="/botany/tropisms">Tropisms simulator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="apical">Auxin and apical dominance</h2>
  <p>
    The growing <strong>shoot tip</strong> makes auxin, which is pumped cell by cell down the stem (polar transport). As it
    passes each node it keeps the <strong>side (axillary) buds</strong> dormant. This is <strong>apical dominance</strong>,
    and it is why many plants grow one main stem. Cut off the tip and the auxin supply stops: the buds below are released
    and grow into side shoots. In 1933 Kenneth Thimann and Folke Skoog showed that putting auxin on the cut stump keeps
    the buds dormant, so auxin really does stand in for the missing tip. Cytokinin from the roots works the other way,
    promoting bud growth: the balance between the two decides which buds grow.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gibberellins">Gibberellins</h2>
  <p>
    Gibberellins make stems grow taller, mainly by making cells <strong>elongate</strong> (and, in some tissues, divide).
    They were discovered in Japan through the “foolish seedling” disease, in which a fungus that makes gibberellin causes
    rice to grow tall and weak. Plants that make little gibberellin are dwarfs; Mendel’s short peas had a mutation in a
    gibberellin-making enzyme, and the “Green Revolution” semi-dwarf wheats respond less to gibberellin, so they put more
    into grain and less into straw. In a germinating cereal grain, the embryo releases gibberellin, which makes the
    <strong>aleurone layer</strong> produce amylase to break down stored starch into sugar for the seedling. The
    <a href="/botany/seed-germination">Seed Germination simulator</a> looks at the conditions a seed needs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cytokinins">Cytokinins</h2>
  <p>
    Cytokinins, made mainly in root tips and carried up in the xylem, promote <strong>cell division</strong> together
    with auxin. They encourage buds and shoots to grow and slow the ageing of leaves, keeping them green. In 1957
    Folke Skoog and Carlos Miller grew tobacco tissue on a jelly containing both hormones: a high auxin-to-cytokinin ratio
    made the tissue form roots, a low ratio made shoots, and a balance gave an undifferentiated lump called a
    <strong>callus</strong>. The same idea is used today to clone plants in the laboratory.
  </p>

  <h2 id="aba">Abscisic acid and drought</h2>
  <p>
    When roots sense drying soil, plants make more <strong>abscisic acid</strong> in their roots and leaves. In the leaves,
    ABA makes the guard cells lose potassium ions and water, so they go limp and the <strong>stomata close</strong>,
    saving water within minutes. ABA also slows shoot growth during stress, and it keeps seeds and buds
    <strong>dormant</strong> until conditions are right. Gibberellin and ABA act against each other in seeds: the balance
    between them decides whether a seed stays dormant or germinates.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ethylene">Ethylene and ripening</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ethylene</strong> (C₂H₄) is a simple gas. In fruits such as tomatoes, bananas and apples, ripening is
    triggered by a burst of ethylene, and ethylene makes the fruit produce even more ethylene. That positive feedback is
    why one ripe fruit can ripen others nearby, and why “one bad apple spoils the barrel”. Ethylene also promotes leaf and
    fruit fall (abscission) and ageing, and slows stem elongation. Blocking ethylene, for example with the gas
    1-methylcyclopropene, keeps fruit fresh for longer in storage.
  </p>

  <h2 id="interactions">Hormones work together</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Auxin vs cytokinin</strong>: auxin from the tip keeps buds dormant, cytokinin from the roots releases them; their ratio also decides roots or shoots in tissue culture.</li>
    <li><strong>Gibberellin vs ABA</strong>: gibberellin promotes germination and growth, ABA promotes dormancy and stress responses.</li>
    <li><strong>Auxin and gibberellin</strong> work together to make stem cells elongate.</li>
    <li><strong>Auxin triggers ethylene</strong>: very high auxin makes tissues produce ethylene, which then slows elongation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Growth is regulated by this network of interactions, not by any single hormone acting alone.</p>

  <h2 id="dose">Dose, tissue and timing</h2>
  <p>
    Hormones are not on/off switches. The same concentration of auxin that makes a stem grow faster strongly inhibits root
    elongation, because roots are far more sensitive: their best concentration is roughly a ten-thousandth of the stem’s.
    The response also depends on the age of the tissue and on the environment: ABA only rises during drought, and
    ripening needs a fruit that is mature enough to respond to ethylene.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Why it matters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Seedless grapes</strong> are sprayed with gibberellin to make larger, looser bunches.</li>
    <li><strong>Brewing</strong>: gibberellin speeds up the malting of barley, when starch is turned into sugar.</li>
    <li><strong>Fruit supply</strong>: bananas are picked green, shipped, then ripened with ethylene; apples are stored with ethylene blockers.</li>
    <li><strong>Pruning</strong>: pinching out shoot tips makes plants such as basil and fuchsias bushy.</li>
    <li><strong>Micropropagation</strong>: orchids, bananas and potatoes are cloned in tissue culture using auxin and cytokinin.</li>
    <li><strong>Crop yields</strong>: semi-dwarf cereals with altered gibberellin responses stand up better and produce more grain.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Each hormone has one job.”</strong> Every hormone affects many processes, and most processes are controlled by several hormones.</li>
    <li><strong>“More hormone always means more growth.”</strong> Many responses have an optimum; too much auxin inhibits roots and triggers ethylene.</li>
    <li><strong>“Plant hormones are made in glands.”</strong> Plants have no glands; many tissues make hormones.</li>
    <li><strong>“Ethylene is a liquid sprayed on fruit.”</strong> Ethylene is a gas that fruit makes itself.</li>
    <li><strong>“Cutting the tip kills the plant’s growth.”</strong> It removes apical dominance, so side shoots grow instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the simulator simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The plant is a generic, two-dimensional herb that grows two days every second, and each hormone is described by one
    number relative to normal. Responses follow simple saturating or optimum-shaped curves chosen to give realistic
    directions, not measured values. Real hormones are made, moved, stored and broken down in ways the model ignores, and
    hormones act through receptors and signalling chains inside cells. Drought is modelled only through ABA, cytokinin and
    reduced growth. The auxin chart shows the classic textbook curves, and the tissue culture follows the auxin-to-cytokinin
    rule of Skoog and Miller; real results vary between species.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Five main hormone groups (auxin, gibberellins, cytokinins, ABA and ethylene) coordinate plant growth and development.</li>
    <li>Auxin from the shoot tip causes apical dominance; cytokinin from the roots counteracts it.</li>
    <li>Gibberellins lengthen stems and mobilise food stores in seeds; ABA closes stomata and keeps seeds dormant.</li>
    <li>Ethylene is a gas that ripens fruit through positive feedback and promotes leaf fall.</li>
    <li>Responses depend on concentration, tissue, developmental stage, environment and the balance with other hormones.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
